[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#C0392B]Before You Buy[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
RTP-Pro is a specialist tool. A few things to confirm before you purchase. If any of these are a hard [B]no[/B] for you, this probably isn't the right plugin for your server, and I'd rather you know that now than after a refund ticket.

[LIST]
[*][B]Java 21 or newer[/B] on your host. This one is non-negotiable (REQ-RTP-SYS-001).
[*][B]Paper, Folia, or Spigot[/B] (including officially-supported Bukkit-compatibility launchers [B]Arclight[/B] and [B]Mohist[/B] for Forge / NeoForge servers). Fabric isn't yet functional in this beta and is planned for a later release; a native Forge adapter is not planned and is lower priority than multi-server / proxy support.
[*][B]Comfortable editing YAML[/B] and reading a wiki. There's no GUI by design, configuration is plain text files you can version and diff.
[*][B]Willing to read the admin guide before asking for help.[/B] See the Support Policy below, this one is enforced.
[*][B]Running a real server,[/B] not a 2-player LAN world. The engineering here targets networks where teleport latency and TPS stability add up to real cost. For a friends-only SMP it is overkill.
[*][B]OK with AI-assisted development[/B] in the authoring loop. See the note at the bottom of this page. Every shipped line is human-reviewed, but the tooling is disclosed.
[/LIST]

If all six are a yes, read on. If any are a no, EssentialsX `/rtp` and HuskHomes RTP are solid free alternatives that will probably serve you better.

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#2980B9]Core Architectural Pillars[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
RTP was rebuilt from scratch to fix the performance and reliability problems that show up in most teleport plugins once you scale past a handful of players. The design rests on five things:

[LIST]
[*][B]Deterministic selection.[/B] A spiral mapping algorithm replaces lag-inducing "re-roll" guessing, and gives you fast, uniform player distribution even in massive worlds.
[*][B]Platform-native performance.[/B] Not just compatible with modern servers, optimized for them. Spigot, Paper, and Folia each get the threading model that actually fits them.
[*][B]Active resource watchdog.[/B] A background sweep reclaims chunk tickets from abandoned teleports via WeakReferences, so stalled tasks can't pin resources and no chunk stays force-loaded past its reservation window.
[*][B]Direct region I/O pre-filter.[/B] On Spigot and Folia, `.mca` region files are parsed off-tick on a background pool. That gives Paper-grade safety evaluation on Spigot, and skips per-candidate Region-Thread hops on Folia. The same reader resolves biomes off-tick, including custom namespaced biomes like Iris.
[*][B]Admin-driven background mapping.[/B] The `/rtp scan` lifecycle (`start` / `pause` / `resume` / `reset` / `cancel`) lets an operator walk the spiral during idle periods, applying the same off-tick safety evaluation so bad-sector learning and observed biomes accumulate without waiting for player traffic. A fully automatic self-warming sampler is on the roadmap for `3.0.0` final.
[/LIST]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#16A085]Chunk Loading, By Platform[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
Chunk I/O is the single biggest source of teleport lag. Most RTP plugins treat every platform the same and end up paying the cost of the [I]worst[/I] one. I wasn't willing to do that. Here is what actually happens when a candidate chunk is evaluated on your server:

[LIST]
[*][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Paper, fully async.[/COLOR][/B] Paper's native `World#getChunkAtAsync` returns a `CompletableFuture<Chunk>`, and RTP uses it directly. Safety evaluation runs off-tick, with no pre-filter and — uniquely on Paper — no main-thread fallback. This is the reference platform.
[*][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Paper forks (Leaf, Leaves, Purpur, Pufferfish, Airplane, …).[/COLOR][/B] Paper-derived forks run the Paper code path and inherit fully-async chunk loading. Use the Paper/Spigot build; no special configuration needed. Folia-derived forks similarly run the Folia code path. ([I]Note: [B]Leaf[/B] and [B]Leaves[/B] are separate projects despite the similar names — both are supported on the Paper code path.[/I])
[*][B][COLOR=#2980B9]Folia, off-tick pre-filter plus the native Region Scheduler.[/COLOR][/B] Folia's chunk API is Region-Thread-bound, which normally costs a scheduler hop per candidate. RTP runs its Anvil read-only pre-filter on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` before the Region Scheduler, so rejected candidates never hop a thread. Confirmed candidates load through Folia's native async API, and teleports dispatch through the Entity Scheduler.
[*][B][COLOR=#E67E22]Spigot, where the Anvil pre-filter does most of the heavy lifting.[/COLOR][/B] Vanilla Spigot has no `CompletableFuture`-returning async chunk API (that overload is a Paper addition), so without mitigation every candidate would drive a synchronous main-thread `getChunkAt`. RTP parses `.mca` region files off-tick instead. `isAir`, `isSafe`, surface-height, and sky-light checks all run on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` against an `AnvilChunkView`. The practical result is Paper-class throughput on Spigot, without having to run a Paper fork.
[*][B][COLOR=#E67E22]Spigot forks (Universe Spigot, …).[/COLOR][/B] Spigot-derived forks without Paper's async chunk API run the Spigot code path (off-tick Anvil pre-filter + bounded main-thread fallback). If your fork is Paper-based, see the Paper-forks bullet above.
[*][B][COLOR=#C0392B]Honest caveats on the Spigot and Folia fallback path.[/COLOR][/B] The Anvil pre-filter is a data source, not a universal gate. It falls through to the platform's native chunk API in two cases: when the chunk is already loaded (live data wins), and when the probe returns [I]unknown[/I] (no region file, unsupported data version, decode error, or un-populated). On Spigot the fallback is one on-tick `getChunkAt`. On Folia it's one Region-Scheduler hop. Custom generators like Iris, Terra, and datapacks are [I]not[/I] a fallback trigger, since populated `.mca` palettes are read directly, preserving modded and namespaced IDs that the Bukkit enum would otherwise collapse.
[*][B][COLOR=#8E44AD]Upgrade-drift proof biome filtering.[/COLOR][/B] When you bump your Paper or Folia server across a Minecraft version, Mojang's seed-based biome assignment can change for coordinates already written to disk. The Anvil-first biome read — applied on every Bukkit-family platform — keeps the `.mca` palette authoritative for populated chunks, so the biome the player actually lands in is the biome your `biomes:` allow-list was written against. No re-pregen, no re-tuning after an upgrade.
[*][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Mohist and Arclight, officially supported.[/COLOR][/B] Mohist (Forge ↔ Bukkit) and Arclight (Forge / NeoForge ↔ Bukkit) are first-class supported platforms — use the Spigot/Paper build of RTP and the Spigot code path applies (off-tick Anvil pre-filter + bounded main-thread fallback). This is the recommended way to run RTP on a Forge or NeoForge server.
[*][B][COLOR=#8E44AD]Fabric: planned support, not functional in `3.0.0-beta.1`.[/COLOR][/B] The `rtp-fabric` adapter ships in-tree but `/rtp` does not yet teleport on Fabric. Don't deploy on Fabric in this beta — Fabric is planned for a later release.
[/LIST]

[B]Operator takeaway:[/B] Paper and Folia run chunk I/O fully off-tick by design. On vanilla Spigot the Anvil pre-filter covers the common case (any unloaded chunk already populated on disk, vanilla or custom-generated), which is the vast majority of candidates. The rare main-thread fallback is bounded by your configured tick-budget, and no platform silently blocks the tick loop without reporting it.

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#2C3E50]Performance vs. Other RTP Plugins[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
[I]How RTP-Pro stacks up against the most-deployed RTP plugins on the three Bukkit-family server architectures it actually targets. The matrix below is the [B]"where it works and how well"[/B] view; the metrics block below it is the [B]"how fast"[/B] view. Forge/NeoForge (via Arclight or Mohist) inherits the Spigot column. Multi-server / proxy and Fabric are out of scope here — Fabric is planned, multi-server is on the roadmap.[/I]

[B]Confidence legend:[/B] 🧪 = measured locally on the RTP benchmark harness · 📖 = read from the plugin's published docs / resource page · ❓ = inferred from the plugin's known architecture, not yet verified.

[B]Architecture support matrix[/B]
[TABLE]
[TR][TD][B]Plugin[/B][/TD][TD][B]Spigot[/B][/TD][TD][B]Paper (and forks)[/B][/TD][TD][B]Folia[/B][/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD][B]RTP-Pro 3.0.0-beta.1[/B][/TD][TD]✅ 🧪 Off-tick Anvil pre-filter; rare bounded main-thread fallback.[/TD][TD]✅ 🧪 Fully async via native `getChunkAtAsync`; no fallback path.[/TD][TD]✅ 🧪 Region Scheduler + off-tick pre-filter; no 1-tick stalls.[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]BetterRTP[/TD][TD]⚠️ 📖 Synchronous chunk load on miss; TPS spikes during generation are widely reported.[/TD][TD]⚠️ 📖 Uses Paper async where available, but no off-tick safety pre-filter.[/TD][TD]✅ 🧪 Functional on Folia 1.21.11 (own pipeline, 100 % success); p99 ~1.2 s — region scheduler absorbs the per-attempt cost.[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]EzRTP[/TD][TD]❌ 🧪 Loads on Spigot 1.20.1 but its async update-checker throws `NoSuchMethodError` on the Paper-only `JavaPlugin#getPluginMeta()` ([I]SpigotUpdateChecker:28[/I]); core teleport path otherwise statistics-driven, no Anvil pre-filter — sync chunk load on miss.[/TD][TD]✅ 📖 Built-in percentile/timing metrics on Paper.[/TD][TD]❓ 📖 Not advertised as Folia-aware.[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]AsyRTP[/TD][TD]❌ 🧪 Fails to enable on Spigot 1.20.1 — `StartupBanner.print` calls the Paper-only `JavaPlugin#getPluginMeta()` during `onEnable`, throwing `NoSuchMethodError` before the plugin initializes. Effectively Paper/Folia-only despite the name.[/TD][TD]✅ 📖 Async location search.[/TD][TD]✅ 📖 Region-thread-safe; explicit Folia target.[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]SorekillRTP[/TD][TD]⚠️ ❓ Functional, but the design centre is Redis-backed cross-server, not single-server perf.[/TD][TD]⚠️ ❓ Same.[/TD][TD]❓ 📖 Folia compatibility not specifically documented.[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]AdvancedRTP[/TD][TD]⚠️ 📖 Safety-first; trades speed for ground/biome detection. Sync chunk load on candidate evaluation.[/TD][TD]⚠️ 📖 Same path on Paper.[/TD][TD]❌ 📖 No documented Folia support.[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]JakesRTP[/TD][TD]⚠️ 📖 Async via `prefer-sync-tp: false`; configurable destination cache (`cache-locations`).[/TD][TD]✅ 📖 Same async path on Paper; widely deployed.[/TD][TD]❌ 📖 No native Folia adapter (community forks vary).[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]EssentialsX `/rtp`[/TD][TD]✅ 📖 Works; main-thread chunk load on each candidate.[/TD][TD]✅ 📖 Works; same path, no async chunk API use.[/TD][TD]❌ 📖 Folia not supported by EssentialsX core.[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]HuskHomes RTP[/TD][TD]✅ 📖 Works; bundled with the broader homes/teleport suite.[/TD][TD]✅ 📖 Same path on Paper.[/TD][TD]✅ 🧪 Functional on Folia 1.21.11 (100 % success, p99 ~900 ms); the no-queue Spigot/Paper failure mode largely disappears on Folia.[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

[B]Measured performance — head-to-head, three platforms[/B] [I](2 OPed real clients spamming `/rtp` back-to-back, queues enabled where the plugin offers them, cooldowns/delays zeroed. Full methodology, raw CSVs, and reproduction harness: [URL=https://github.com/dailystruggle/RTP/tree/main/helpers/StressTestRTP]`helpers/StressTestRTP/`[/URL].)[/I]

[I]Four familiar metrics per row:[/I]
[LIST]
[*][B]Throughput[/B] — successful `/rtp`s per second, sustained across the phase. Higher is better.
[*][B]MSPT p99[/B] — worst 1-in-100 main-thread tick cost during the phase, in milliseconds. Lower is better; under ~50 ms means the spike doesn't drop a tick.
[*][B]Min TPS[/B] — lowest server TPS observed during the phase. 20.00 means the server never hiccupped.
[*][B]CPU / TP[/B] — main-thread CPU spent per successful teleport, in milliseconds. The "how much MSPT does each teleport cost" number.
[/LIST]

[B]Paper 1.21.11[/B] — canonical head-to-head dataset (Paper is the recommended platform).
[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Plugin[/TH][TH]Throughput (TP/s)[/TH][TH]MSPT p99 (ms)[/TH][TH]Min TPS[/TH][TH]CPU / TP (ms)[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD][B]🧪 RTP-Pro[/B][/TD][TD][B]19.8[/B][/TD][TD][B]4[/B][/TD][TD][B]20.00[/B][/TD][TD][B]16.9[/B][/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]🧪 JakesRTP[/TD][TD]20.0[/TD][TD]70[/TD][TD]20.00[/TD][TD]26.0[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]🧪 BetterRTP[/TD][TD]7.1[/TD][TD]771[/TD][TD]20.00[/TD][TD]53.6[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]🧪 HuskHomes[/TD][TD]6.2[/TD][TD]335[/TD][TD]20.00[/TD][TD]52.2[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]
[I]Reading: RTP-Pro serves the same throughput as the next-best competitor (JakesRTP) at roughly [B]17×[/B] lower worst-case MSPT and [B]35 %[/B] lower CPU per teleport. BetterRTP and HuskHomes both spend 3× the CPU per teleport for ⅓ the throughput — and their tick spikes (335–771 ms p99) are large enough to be felt on a busy server even though average TPS holds at 20.[/I]

[B]Spigot 1.20.1[/B]
[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Plugin[/TH][TH]Throughput (TP/s)[/TH][TH]MSPT p99 (ms)[/TH][TH]Min TPS[/TH][TH]CPU / TP (ms)[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD][B]🧪 RTP-Pro[/B][/TD][TD][B]1.52[/B][/TD][TD][B]3[/B][/TD][TD][B]6.4[/B][/TD][TD]572[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]🧪 JakesRTP[/TD][TD]1.04[/TD][TD]2 252[/TD][TD]—†[/TD][TD]—†[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]🧪 BetterRTP[/TD][TD]1.33[/TD][TD]3 790[/TD][TD]2.18[/TD][TD]584[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]🧪 HuskHomes[/TD][TD]0.93[/TD][TD]4 939[/TD][TD]2.59[/TD][TD]868[/TD][/TR]
