[/TABLE]
[I]Reading: on Folia, "MSPT" means the per-region tick — Folia's whole point is that bad regions don't stall the server. RTP-Pro still wins by ~2.5× on throughput and ~7× on worst-region tick cost, because the off-tick Anvil pre-filter runs before the region scheduler ever sees the candidate.[/I]

[B]Bottom line.[/B] On Paper (recommended) RTP-Pro delivers the same throughput as the next-best competitor at a fraction of the MSPT and CPU cost. On Spigot, the off-tick Anvil pre-filter keeps the worst tick three orders of magnitude smaller than non-queued plugins. On Folia, the same pattern holds — and only RTP-Pro hits double-digit TP/s.

[B]Memory and server TPS.[/B] RTP-Pro's queue is bounded (`cacheCap` entries, default 10, recommended 100 on Paper) and each entry is a small POJO — multi-megabyte queue costs on a tuned configuration are not observed. Server TPS held at [B]20.00[/B] throughout all Paper and Folia runs across every plugin in the roster; on Spigot, every plugin saturated to 3.5–4.5 TPS regardless of choice (platform chunk-gen ceiling, not plugin-specific) — RTP-Pro just spends those ticks doing fewer things.

‡ Paper RTP-Pro row is reproduced across two consecutive runs (n=2, within a few percent of each other). Other rows are n=1 on a single rig — read as "this rig, this version, this configuration", not as universal claims.

[B]Methodology and honest caveats.[/B]
[LIST]
[*]Architecture-matrix verdicts marked 📖 are read from the plugin's own published documentation; ❓ verdicts are inferred from the plugin's known architecture. 🧪 verdicts in both tables are measured against the in-tree [URL=https://github.com/dailystruggle/RTP/tree/main/helpers/StressTestRTP]StressTestRTP[/URL] harness on the same hardware.
[*]Two real OPed clients only; higher-concurrency saturation curves require a bot harness and are pending future work. The 2-client number is a floor, not a ceiling.
[*]Numbers are not predictions for [I]your[/I] server. Hardware, view distance, world generation state, other plugins, and player count will all move them. The harness, raw CSVs, and per-run analyses (with retractions and reproducibility bands) are public so you can re-run on your rig.
[*]Competitor plugins update frequently. This comparison reflects observed behavior at the time of writing — corrections welcome via GitHub issue, with a link to the contradicting documentation or a reproduction.
[*]Feature breadth, GUI, and claim-plugin integration are [I]not[/I] benchmarked here. Several competitors trade speed for those features, and that's a legitimate choice — the table is about throughput and platform fit, not feature parity.
[/LIST]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#E67E22]The Engineering: A Deeper Dive[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]

[B]Algorithmic spatial memory, instead of brute-force guessing[/B]
Without spatial indexing, teleportation is a guessing game, and the numbers are ugly. A typical Overworld is only around 45% safely teleportable[SUP]†[/SUP] (oceans, rivers, ravines, and steep terrain eat the rest), the Nether is dominated by lava seas and wall-to-wall stone, and the End is [I]almost entirely[/I] void. Standard RTPs pay full chunk-load cost to discover this one candidate at a time, forever.
[SIZE=2][I]† Local-profile estimate from a vanilla 1.21 seed set; Nether and End ratios are still qualitative. Anonymous opt-in telemetry is on the roadmap to source this number properly — see [I]Roadmap[/I] below.[/I][/SIZE]
RTP plots your world's geometry as it evaluates candidates. When it hits a massive ocean or an unsafe biome, it remembers that sector and shrinks its searchable area, so known bad sectors never get loaded again. The longer it runs, the faster it gets — and the admin-driven `/rtp scan` lifecycle keeps that learning going during idle periods.
[B]Persistence across restarts:[/B] learned bad-sector state survives a JVM bounce — `MemoryShape.save` / `load` write the per-region spatial memory to disk on shutdown and reload it on startup, alongside the configuration cache (compiled safety sets, region geometry). H2, SQLite, MySQL, and PostgreSQL backends are additionally supported for shared multi-server state.

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#27AE60]Feature Set & Integrations[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]

[B]Full Feature Suite:[/B]
[LIST]
[*]Any number of teleportation regions per world
[*]Per-region shapes (Square, Circle, Rectangle) with configurable radius, center, and weighting
[*]Per-region vertical adjustors (Linear, Jump) to navigate complex terrain
[*]Optional sky light checks, world permission requirements, and unique placement guarantees
[*]Configurable safety radius, platform creation, and invulnerability timers
[*]Fully async chunk loading on Paper and Folia; off-tick Anvil pre-filter on Spigot (see platform breakdown above)
[*][B]Per-player isolated queues[/B] alongside a global queue, so one player's bad luck never starves another's teleport
[*][B]Administrative world-scan lifecycle[/B] (`start` / `pause` / `resume` / `reset` / `cancel`) to pre-populate spatial memory without teleporting players
[*][B]Persistent learned state[/B] across restarts via H2 / SQLite
[/LIST]
[B]Supported Claim Plugins:[/B]
Native, thread-safe integrations for the following are built into the plugin (no separate addon download required, folded in per ADR-019):
[LIST]
[*]Factions (and derivatives)
[*]GriefDefender
[*]GriefPrevention
[*]HuskTowns
[*]Lands
[*]RedProtect
[*]TownyAdvanced
[*]WorldGuard
[/LIST]
[B]Granular Configuration:[/B]
RTP exposes its core pipelines through modular configuration, allowing you to tailor the engine to your hardware.
[LIST]
[*]Configurable per-tick time budgets for the teleport-task pipeline (and per-tick task-count caps on Folia's region-bound pipe).
[*]Customize unsafe materials and safety radii, using a full [B]token grammar[/B] (see below), not just flat material names.
[*]Apply dimension locks and unique safety configs (e.g., Nether vs. Overworld).
[*]Establish variable pricing for different teleport types.
[*]Complete localization and player-facing text isolation.
[*][I]Note:[/I] the [B]`/rtp config`[/B] command is shipped as [B]experimental[/B] in `3.0.0-beta.1`. Subcommand surface, argument grammar, and write-back semantics may change before `3.0.0` final. Prefer editing the YAML files directly for production until the experimental label is dropped.
[/LIST]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#16A085]Safety List Grammar: Block Tags, Block States, and Wildcards[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
`safety.yml` has a first-class token grammar. You aren't limited to enumerating every `Material` name anymore, you can target entire vanilla block tags and match on block-state properties. All five shapes can be mixed freely in `unsafeBlocks` (and `airBlocks`).

[LIST]
[*][B]Plain material:[/B] `LAVA`, `MAGMA_BLOCK`. Exactly what you'd expect.
[*][B]Material with a state predicate:[/B] `OAK_SLAB[waterlogged=true]` matches oak slabs [I]only when waterlogged[/I]. Multiple predicates are AND-ed, like `OAK_SLAB[waterlogged=true,type=top]`.
[*][B]Vanilla block tag:[/B] `#minecraft:leaves`, `#minecraft:fire`, `#minecraft:campfires`. The grammar is accepted today; live-registry expansion at config-load time (so the set updates automatically when Mojang adds new blocks to a tag) lands in `3.0.0` final — see [I]Roadmap[/I] below. Use explicit material names alongside tag tokens during the beta.
[*][B]Tag with a state predicate:[/B] `#minecraft:slabs[waterlogged=true]`, i.e. "any slab, but only when waterlogged". No more manually listing every slab variant.
[*][B]Wildcard with a state predicate:[/B] `*[waterlogged=true]`, i.e. "any block at all, matched when `waterlogged=true`". One line replaces the entire waterloggable-block enumeration (iron bars, lanterns, chains, fences, all of them).
[/LIST]

[B]Key properties:[/B]
[LIST]
[*][B]Fail-open on unknown tags and properties.[/B] Missing tags on older MC versions silently reduce coverage rather than breaking startup, which keeps your config portable across Minecraft versions.
[*][B]Never silent on malformed tokens.[/B] Typos like `OAK_SLAB[waterlogged=true` (missing closing bracket) surface as a single `[WARNING] [safety.yml] rejected token '…': <reason>` in console, so you always know what was dropped.
[*][B]Zero hot-path cost when unused.[/B] The evaluator only extracts block-state properties when a token in your config actually needs them, and plain-material configs pay nothing.
[*][B]Platform-portable tag resolution.[/B] Bukkit platforms read tags from the live registry (`Bukkit.getTag`), and the standalone `rtp-tags` module can parse tag JSON directly from data packs and jars for future non-Bukkit platforms.
[/LIST]

[B]Example: the new default `safety.yml` is roughly 25% shorter.[/B]
[LIST]
[*]`FIRE` + `SOUL_FIRE` → `#minecraft:fire`
[*]`CAMPFIRE` + `SOUL_CAMPFIRE` → `#minecraft:campfires`
[*]Every waterloggable block → `*[waterlogged=true]`
[*]Dozens of decorative air-safe plants → `#minecraft:flowers`, `#minecraft:saplings`, `#minecraft:crops`, `#minecraft:rails`, `#minecraft:buttons`, `#minecraft:pressure_plates`, sign tags, …
[/LIST]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#D35400]Roadmap: Known Limitations and Planned Work[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
I don't hide the rough edges. Here is what I already know is incomplete in `3.0.0-beta.1`, along with the direction each one is heading. The full tiered roadmap lives in the repository, this is the short version.

[LIST]
[*][B]Vanilla Spigot still pays a rare main-thread fallback.[/B] When the Anvil pre-filter can't resolve a chunk, one on-tick load fills the gap. Planned: expose per-minute fallback counters so operators can see the real cost, and ship an optional strict mode that skips the fallback entirely for zero main-thread time.
[*][B]Folia candidates can still pay one scheduler hop.[/B] Only for candidates the pre-filter couldn't resolve, but the cost is currently unmeasured. Planned: publish actual p50/p95 numbers, and look at amortising the hop across adjacent candidates.
[*][B]Un-populated chunks always fall through to a live load.[/B] This is correct by design, since the live load is the authoritative safety net, but telemetry doesn't distinguish this path from other fallbacks today. Planned: a dedicated attribution bucket so operators can tell these apart at a glance, and documented guidance on warming populations ahead of time via the admin scan lifecycle.
[*][B]Fabric is not functional in this beta, but is planned for a later release.[/B] Known blockers: scheduled-task processor parity, permissions integration, full Brigadier command tree. Planned: resolve the remaining blockers and ship Fabric alongside the Bukkit family in a future release. Forge / NeoForge remains lower priority than multi-server / proxy work; the officially-supported path today is Arclight or Mohist with the Spigot/Paper build.
[*][B]Only one marketing number is sourced so far.[/B] The ~45% Overworld-safe figure above comes from local profiling of a vanilla 1.21 seed set; Nether and End ratios are still qualitative. Planned: anonymous opt-in usage telemetry and a published reference benchmark, so every figure on this page can cite a real number.
[*][B]Demonstration videos predate this release.[/B] The clips below still illustrate the learning and queue principles accurately, but the numbers shown aren't current-release benchmarks. Planned: fresh footage recorded against `3.0.0` before the `-beta` tag comes off.
[/LIST]

[I]Everything above is a known issue with a plan — not a surprise. If you hit something that isn't on this list, I want to know about it, so please open an issue on the GitHub repo linked below.[/I]

[CENTER][COLOR=#D1D1D1]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/COLOR][/CENTER]

[SIZE=5][B][COLOR=#9B59B6]FAQ[/COLOR][/B][/SIZE]
[B]Q: This plugin responds much faster than I'm used to. Why am I not waiting for chunk loading?[/B]
A: When I say optimized, I mean it. In most cases, the pre-warmed caching system has a safe location ready to go before you even type the command.

[B]Q: How do I set up teleportation for different worlds?[/B]
A: Check the wiki for details on configuring regions and worlds. The logic follows a clear hierarchy: player's current world -> world's target region -> region's target world.

[B]Q: What's the best setup for performance?[/B]
A: The plugin is optimized for Paper and Folia servers. Because it relies on pre-loading, it works best when you have memory to spare for the location cache.

[B]Q: Does RTP work with Iris, Terra, or other custom-generator worlds?[/B]
A: Yes, this was a deliberate 3.0 design goal. The Anvil pre-filter reads `.mca` palette data directly, so populated custom-generator chunks evaluate off-tick like vanilla. That's actually [I]strictly more accurate[/I] than the live Bukkit view, which collapses modded and Iris-native IDs to vanilla cousins. Un-populated chunks fall through to the live load as the authoritative safety net.

[B]Q: What happens to my existing `safety.yml` when I upgrade?[/B]
A: Existing plain-material entries keep working unchanged, since the new grammar is strictly additive. No manual conversion is required on upgrade.

